;; One mutation per invariant. `:find` must occur EXACTLY ONCE in `:file`.
;;
;; ## SCOPE
;;
;; Covers the 2026-08-18 `.clj` → `.cljc` conversion only: that the registry
;; is compiled in rather than read at runtime, that the generated projection
;; cannot drift from the EDN unnoticed, and that a registry handed in as nil
;; does not flatten into a complete-looking empty lookup. It does NOT cover
;; maturity, readiness, execution-plan, the technology stack resolution or
;; the contacts directory — those have 1,153 assertions above and no
;; mutations here, so a clean run means "the portability invariants are
;; measured" and NOT "this library is measured".
;;
;; ⚠ These mutations drive `kbb -M:test`. The `:cljs` half is exercised
;; by `test/run_portable.cljk`, which this harness does not run — a mutation
;; breaking only a cljs branch survives here. In `kotoba-lang/technology` one
;; did exactly that, and it was retargeted rather than deleted.
;; ⚠ The first form of the drift mutation renamed the `def` itself, and the
;; harness scored it UNMEASURED rather than killed — correctly: the rename
;; broke the require, the namespace would not load, and a suite that cannot
;; start has not been shown to notice anything. It is retargeted at a VALUE
;; inside the data, which is the drift a hand-edit actually looks like. The
;; misfire is recorded because "unmeasured" and "killed" are different
;; results and a table that hid the difference would be the defect this
;; harness exists to catch.
[{:id :the-projection-cannot-drift-unnoticed
  :invariant "the generated namespace matches the EDN a human edits"
  :file "src/kotoba/iso3166/embedded.cljk"
  :find ":code \"JPN\" :name \"Japan\""
  :replace ":code \"JPN\" :name \"Japan-DRIFTED\""}

 {:id :the-drift-check-refuses-when-it-cannot-read
  :invariant "a check that could not run must not report a pass"
  :file "test/kotoba/iso3166_test.cljk"
  :find "#?(:clj (try (slurp path) (catch Exception _ nil))"
  :replace "#?(:clj (try (slurp (str path \".missing\")) (catch Exception _ nil))"}

 {:id :by-code-does-not-invent-an-empty-index
  :invariant "(into {} …) over nil yields {} — an index over no data"
  :file "src/kotoba/iso3166.cljk"
  :find "  ([reg] (when-let [cs (countries reg)]\n           (into {} (map (juxt :code identity)) cs))))"
  :replace "  ([reg] (into {} (map (juxt :code identity)) (countries reg))))"}

 ;; A mutation was written and REMOVED here, with its reason, because
 ;; deleting one to clean a table is what this harness exists to prevent:
 ;;
 ;;   :countries-reads-the-registry-given-to-it — replacing
 ;;   `([] (countries (registry)))` with `([] (:iso3166 (registry)))`
 ;;   reddened nothing, and it is a semantic no-op: the one-arg body IS
 ;;   `(:iso3166 reg)`, so the two are identical for every input including
 ;;   nil. The same no-op appeared in `kotoba-lang/technology` and was
 ;;   removed there for the same reason. The change is still worth having —
 ;;   one path instead of two, so a guard added to the one-arg form cannot
 ;;   be skipped by the zero-arg one — but it is not an invariant anything
 ;;   can observe today.

 {:id :the-registry-is-the-embedded-one
  :invariant "the library reads the projection and not something else"
  :file "src/kotoba/iso3166.cljk"
  :find "  embedded/registry-data)"
  :replace "  {})"}

 {:id :contacts-come-from-the-embedded-projection
  :invariant "the contacts directory is compiled in too, not read"
  :file "src/kotoba/iso3166.cljk"
  :find "  (:kotoba.iso3166/contacts embedded/contacts-data))"
  :replace "  {})"}]
